1
00:00:00,058 --> 00:00:07,012
A five-minute black-box tour of the traffic signal control prototype, built from the architecture in the assignment answer.

2
00:00:07,491 --> 00:00:12,356
Decide locally on current, synchronised data. Plan, validate and observe centrally.

3
00:00:13,614 --> 00:00:20,743
Twenty intersections, each a real C++ controller process with its own independent safety unit, written in C.

4
00:00:21,218 --> 00:00:27,232
A hardware-in-the-loop bench simulates the street and detectors. Vehicles obey the lamps after the safety unit.

5
00:00:27,685 --> 00:00:32,422
A shared forty-second cycle, offset per column, gives the green wave on the main road.

6
00:00:32,913 --> 00:00:39,740
Blue dots are authenticated channel messages on the peer Ethernet: eight detector readings per controller, once per cycle.

7
00:00:40,221 --> 00:00:49,869
Dashed lines are PTP links where there is no GNSS sky view. All twenty controllers are online at the center, over MQTT with mutual TLS.

8
00:00:50,322 --> 00:00:56,313
Intersection seventeen's cabinet: signal heads, then the controller's mode, phase and cycle position.

9
00:00:56,767 --> 00:01:04,139
It samples at thirteen seconds and decides at fourteen whether to extend green. Zero C++ heap allocations after start-up.

10
00:01:04,665 --> 00:01:10,052
Two CANopen cards serve eight loops: stop bars, and exits for queue and gridlock detection.

11
00:01:10,504 --> 00:01:17,203
Then time hardware, peer channels with sequence numbers, and the MQTT link with its store-and-forward backlog.

12
00:01:17,661 --> 00:01:21,307
Every decision becomes a hash-chained record, uploaded to the center.

13
00:01:21,759 --> 00:01:31,639
Intersection thirteen extended green by six seconds: rule R13-02 fired because upstream intersection eight saw a short gap. A platoon is coming.

14
00:01:32,094 --> 00:01:39,780
The trace shows every input with sample time, sequence and age, the deadline, the release, the Git commit, and the intact hash chain.

15
00:01:40,232 --> 00:01:47,616
Amber and all-red are fixed, an empty side street is skipped, and a gridlock rule holds instead of extending when the exit is blocked.

16
00:01:48,069 --> 00:01:53,224
Faults. Cut loop one at intersection seventeen, and its CANopen card sends an emergency frame.

17
00:01:53,677 --> 00:02:00,086
Only the rule using that reading falls back to its if-missing action; the others keep working, and an alarm goes to the center.

18
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:03,691
A failed side-street detector counts as permanent demand.

19
00:02:04,182 --> 00:02:09,964
Intersection sixteen loses power. Without a watchdog signal, its safety unit flashes it within a second.

20
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:15,912
Seventeen detects the silent neighbour by itself, from the missing messages. No center involved.

21
00:02:16,381 --> 00:02:21,188
Its upstream rule falls back to no extension: background timing, degraded mode.

22
00:02:21,663 --> 00:02:26,133
Lose its own stop-bar detectors as well, and it runs a full local fixed-time plan.

23
00:02:26,622 --> 00:02:29,583
Fixed time: safe, and still serving every approach.

24
00:02:30,049 --> 00:02:39,650
Time has three tiers: GNSS pulse per second, IEEE 1588 PTP from a neighbour, then holdover on the temperature-compensated crystal.

25
00:02:40,103 --> 00:02:44,921
GNSS off at intersection twelve: it becomes a PTP slave.

26
00:02:45,377 --> 00:02:52,157
PTP off too: holdover. A two ppm crystal stays within a hundred milliseconds for almost fourteen hours.

27
00:02:52,630 --> 00:03:00,246
A GNSS spoof of eight hundred milliseconds at intersection seven is rejected, because its PTP peer disagrees.

28
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:05,623
The center only estimates clocks, to plus or minus five seconds. It never sets them.

29
00:03:06,076 --> 00:03:08,340
Now every link to the control center goes down.

30
00:03:08,812 --> 00:03:15,209
The broker publishes each controller's last will. On the street, nothing changes: the center is never in the decision path.

31
00:03:15,688 --> 00:03:19,519
Records queue on flash, and upload later with their original timestamps.

32
00:03:19,987 --> 00:03:23,807
A software bug at intersection nine commands conflicting greens.

33
00:03:24,295 --> 00:03:31,458
The independent safety unit, watching the real lamp outputs, caught it within a hundred and fifty milliseconds and latched flashing.

34
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:38,593
It also checks multiple indications, dark heads, short ambers and the watchdog. Only a technician reset releases it.

35
00:03:39,059 --> 00:03:48,684
A forged and a replayed message on channel sixteen, the red dots: the AES-CMAC exposes the forgery, the sequence number the replay. Both are dropped.

36
00:03:49,138 --> 00:03:54,095
Rules are data, not code: a table, with an if-missing fallback on every rule.

37
00:03:54,585 --> 00:04:01,818
R17-01 now extends at twenty percent occupancy instead of twenty-five, by five seconds instead of four.

38
00:04:02,273 --> 00:04:06,220
Saving commits readable rule text to Git, under the engineer's name.

39
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:15,343
Validation checks all twenty controllers: interpreter bounds, extension limits, buffer load, deadlines, current-cycle data and channel budget.

40
00:04:15,795 --> 00:04:18,581
Replay shows which recorded decisions the edit would change.

41
00:04:19,076 --> 00:04:21,317
Approving your own release is refused.

42
00:04:23,191 --> 00:04:29,112
A second engineer approves; the rules compile to a binary table, signed with Ed25519.

43
00:04:29,567 --> 00:04:35,453
Deployment stages it in the inactive flash slot; the controller verifies the signature and acknowledges.

44
00:04:35,956 --> 00:04:43,537
One signed activation command sets a common time; each controller switches at its next cycle boundary, on its synchronised clock.

45
00:04:43,992 --> 00:04:49,135
Seventeen now runs version two from slot B; version one stays in slot A for rollback.

46
00:04:49,589 --> 00:04:59,167
The monitor shows what the center knows: mode, sync, detectors, neighbours, buffers, security, configuration and safety state, plus every alarm.

47
00:04:59,713 --> 00:05:09,210
Every protocol is real and capturable: logs, CAN dumps, NMEA, the broker log, and Wireshark captures of PTP and channel traffic.

48
00:05:09,663 --> 00:05:15,631
A self-test injects all of these faults automatically: thirty-seven of thirty-seven checks pass.

49
00:05:16,083 --> 00:05:22,805
Only the street, CAN wiring, network switch and clock hardware are simulated. The rest is the real software of the design.
